  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead they had in the second was gone by the fourth, and they never got it back.",
    "what_this_means": "Nine runners left on base. One scored. That's the arithmetic of a two-run loss. Good enough pitching to stay alive, offense that couldn't answer when it had to. Jack Perkins gave them five solid innings with two earned runs, which in most nights wins a game. Instead it became proof that one starter's competence doesn't matter if the lineup goes quiet when runners are in scoring position. The Angels scored twice in the fourth to flip a one-run deficit into a one-run lead. The Athletics had their moment to respond and didn't. They brought the same silence into the eighth. That's the story: pitching held up, bats folded when it mattered. In a division fight already tight at 1.5 back, nights like this are math problems. They're losing reps where they can't afford to lose.",
    "what_to_watch": "Sam Aldegheri pitched a 5.47 ERA last season, so tonight's matchup against him is an opening the lineup has to find. And find cleanly. First pitch at 12:15 PT, same ballpark, chance to recalibrate what went wrong in the offense.",
